# Ignore rules scoped to the slatwall-ts/ subtree. Git applies every pattern below relative
# to this directory, so nothing here reaches the legacy CFML tree.
#
# That scoping is the reason the file exists. The repository-root .gitignore covers the legacy
# application and not one of its fifteen entries covers node_modules, dist, build-meta or
# coverage; closing that gap here rather than there keeps the root file, and the whole CFML tree,
# byte-for-byte unchanged, so this port stays a single additive diff.
#
# The list is five patterns: the four generated directories node_modules/, dist/, build-meta/ and
# coverage/, plus the file pattern .env. Count the patterns below before restating that number here.
# Speculative boilerplate is omitted rather than copied in (AAP §0.7.3 standard 9; §0.8.2 guideline 1).
#
# The four directory names are shared with tsconfig.json "exclude", tsconfig.build.json "outDir" and
# "exclude", eslint.config.mjs "ignores", jest.config.ts "coverageDirectory" and
# "testPathIgnorePatterns", and the output, staging and sourcemap directories in build/esbuild.mjs.
# Every one of those must keep spelling them identically: a name that drifts here leaves a build
# product tracked, and one that drifts in eslint.config.mjs leaves it linted.
#
# The omissions are decisions rather than oversights. .env is the only credential-bearing entry: a
# real .env may hold live database credentials, whereas configuration otherwise reaches the service
# only through the process environment (AAP §0.4.3.5 and §0.8.3.9) and no file in this subtree holds a
# credential. The committed .env.example carries variable names only — required ones blank, optional
# ones commented out, no value of any kind — and is tracked on purpose; see the note on the .env
# entry below. No editor, OS or tool-cache pattern is listed: each would be boilerplate for an
# artifact this subtree never emits.

# Installed dependency tree, materialised by `npm ci` from package-lock.json. The
# lockfile itself is deliberately NOT ignored: a committed lockfile is what makes the
# exact-version resolution reproducible (AAP §0.7.3 standard 5).
node_modules/

# Compiled and bundled output. Both routes to an artifact emit here — `tsc -p
# tsconfig.build.json` (outDir "dist") and `node build/esbuild.mjs` (the bundles) — and build
# products are never committed. `dist/` is the packaged tree: after a build it holds one
# CommonJS bundle per declared Lambda entry point under `handlers/`, the derived production
# `package.json`, and the staged runtime dependency closure under `node_modules/`.
dist/

# Source maps for those bundles, emitted beside `dist/` rather than into it so the packaged tree
# carries only what the runtime loads — the maps are several times the size of the code they
# describe. `build/esbuild.mjs` writes them with `sourcemap: 'external'`, so no
# `sourceMappingURL` comment is written into the JavaScript and relocating a map leaves no
# dangling reference.
#
# It also holds the package while it is being assembled: every artifact is written into
# `build-meta/package-staging/` and `dist/` is then created by a single `rename` of that
# directory, so a build that fails at any step leaves `dist/` absent rather than stale or
# partial. The staging directory is a sibling of `dist/` because `rename` is atomic only within
# one filesystem. Not to be confused with `build/`, which holds the build script and is tracked.
build-meta/

# Jest coverage reports (jest.config.ts, coverageDirectory: 'coverage').
coverage/

# This file is also Prettier's ignore list, and it is the only one: no .prettierignore exists in
# this subtree. Prettier's CLI defaults `--ignore-path` to `[.gitignore, .prettierignore]` and
# skips whichever is absent, so the directories above are already excluded from
# `npx prettier --check .` without a second list to keep in sync. Verified by writing
# deliberately mis-formatted probes into dist/, coverage/ and src/ and running the check once:
# only the src/ probe was reported, which proves both that the generated directories are excluded
# and that the scan was happening at all. `npx prettier package-lock.json` also emits the file
# byte-for-byte, so checking the lockfile is a no-op rather than a churn risk.

# A real .env is a local developer convenience that may hold live database credentials,
# so it never enters git; configuration reaches the service through the environment and
# no credential is hardcoded (AAP §0.8.3.9). The pattern is the exact filename and not a
# wildcard, so the required, committed .env.example — variable names only, no values —
# is never swallowed.
.env
